    kind: work_item  # fcbb
    title: "Forward dataflow slice: decide and implement option (2) or (3)"  # fcbb
    status: todo_soft  # fcbb
    priority: 3  # fcbb
    parent:  # fcbb
    tags:  # fcbb
    - analysis_quality  # fcbb
    before: []  # fcbb
    duplicate_of: []  # fcbb
    not_duplicate_of: []  # fcbb
    pr_ref:  # fcbb
    description: "Longer-term direction for forward dataflow slicing, following the immediate WI-saful option (1) fix (one-hop downstream read admission from writer nodes, implemented in slice.py).\n\nCONTEXT: ADR-0015 §6 describes the intended semantics as 'BFS only follows edges where a write/mutate at the source connects to a read at the destination'. WI-saful (see tracker) shipped option (1) because it was small, contained to slice.py BFS, and cleared the immediate prometheus LOW_DATAFLOW_SLICE_RATIO signal by roughly tripling the dataflow slice. Option (1) has a structural limitation: it only admits read edges that are directly downstream of a writer — it cannot carry a 'this path is in the writer chain' flag further through the graph, so multi-hop dataflow like 'writer → passthrough → reader' misses the reader unless the passthrough is also a writer in its own right.\n\nTWO ALTERNATIVES TO DECIDE BETWEEN:\n\nOption (2) PRINCIPLED: populate edge.meta.dest_access_mode at edge creation time (linker work), then in BFS check both src and dst modes — admit edges where (forward: src in {write,mutate} OR dst in {read,mutate}). This is the 'right' longer-term direction: it makes the access_mode annotation a property of the edge (src->dst transition), not just the source node, and eliminates per-node writer-chain bookkeeping. Cost: touching every linker that creates dataflow-relevant edges (core base.py annotate_dataflow, plus 10+ linkers that call Edge.create with access_mode=...). The dest_access_mode field ALREADY EXISTS in the Edge schema and is referenced by a handful of cgo/ffi/napi/crypto_flow/ipc/ruby_ffi/lua_ffi/yjs_crdt/event_sourcing linkers, but is not populated by the main annotate_dataflow pipeline.\n\nOption (3) STATEFUL: track per-node 'arrived via writer chain?' state in BFS, then admit reads only from writer-chain nodes. Cleaner than option (1) because the 'writer' concept becomes a BFS invariant rather than a precomputed set-membership check. No linker changes needed. Cost: adds an axis to the visited set (visited becomes Set[tuple[node_id, in_writer_chain]]).\n\nDECIDE: which option is correct long-term? The decision must cover:\n  a) Does option (2) actually eliminate the need for option (1)? (Likely yes, if dest_access_mode is populated consistently.)\n  b) Does option (3) handle polyglot linker edges (cgo, ipc, yjs_crdt, event_sourcing) as gracefully as option (2)? These linkers already set dest_access_mode explicitly, suggesting the schema anticipated option (2).\n  c) What is the impact on slice size and accuracy on a real repo (e.g., prometheus)? Re-run the bakeoff after each option and compare slice.dataflow.0 node/edge counts to slice.0.\n  d) What is the migration path from option (1) to the chosen option? Can option (1) be left in place as a fallback, or must it be removed to avoid double-counting?\n\nIMPLEMENTATION: after decision, implement the chosen option, add regression tests (distinct from the WI-saful option (1) tests — the new tests should cover the multi-hop cases that option (1) cannot handle), and validate against the latest deep-bakeoff session. Update ADR-0015 to reflect the final semantics. Reference WI-saful in the PR description for full auditability."  # fcbb
    fields: {}  # fcbb
